WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.759
Hi everyone it's Jerry. This is round 10 from
the 2013 World Chess Championship match between

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:13.879
Anand and Carlsen. Going into this round Carlsen
has a 6 to 3 lead over Anand. And Carlsen

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:17.970
with the white pieces in this one opened with
e4. And for the first time in the match we

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.679
get to see the Sicilian Defense. Keep in mind
Carlsen needs only a draw or a win in order

00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:30.679
to become World Champion. It is a race again
to 6 and a half points. And we see the Rossolimo.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.860
Knight blocks and as a follow-up, d4. If you're
looking for a fairly quiet system in reply

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:43.860
to the Sicilian, bishop b5 line might be something
to look into. Carlsen at this stage in the

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:50.410
match not wanting to complicate the position
too much. So we have this exchange, and soon

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:57.410
as the bishop is questioned, white gives it
up. But has at least inserted c4 so that there's

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:03.650
going to be control over the two potential
pawn breaks--d5 and b5. And we'll see a lot

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:10.650
of emphasis placed on controlling these squares
from here on out. So first knight f6, bishop

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.880
g5. You may not think there's too much difference
between these two moves at this moment but

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.170
there certainly is. By keeping pressure on
the knight, there's not time to fianchetto

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.689
the bishop successfully because you can take
the knight off, damage the structure, and

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:32.630
this knight would be granted a beautiful home
on d5. But if you were to bring your knight

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:39.630
out, well now there's time for black to maybe
consider this. But it's bishop g5, and so

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:46.649
the bishop has to go elsewhere. Knight c3,
bishop e7, castles. Bishop c6 putting pressure

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.959
on e4 and this is a quite common maneuver
out of this variation to drop the queen back.

00:01:52.959 --> 00:01:59.200
There's a few reasons behind that. One it
vacates the d4 square for the knight. It could

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.380
grab the bishop, and it's still keeping a
close eye on the two potential pawn breaks--d5

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:11.380
and b5. Black castles, sure enough the knight
posts in the center, reinforces the structure

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:18.650
with b3. And now we grab that bishop. And
we play our rook to c1. This might be tempting

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:25.560
as well. It's a half-open file, but it doesn't
address this potential pawn break. If rook

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.989
on f to d1, we have b5 and you can't take
cause you would drop the knight. So we need

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:38.989
to first secure our knight position so b5
is no longer a good idea. You could just win

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:46.950
a pawn. Maybe there's even something sneakier.
So we don't do that. b5 is not allowed. Bishop

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:53.849
has a question thrown to it. This is also
a flight square. Knight to d7. There's two

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:58.989
possibilities for this knight now. b6 isn't
really doing anything but tying down the queen

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:05.989
to its defense. Better post for the bishop.
Hard square to argue with. It's very well

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:12.170
centralized and pointing right at g7. There
could maybe one day be another piece converging

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:18.239
on that g7 point. So finally the last rook
gets involved for black. A little flight square

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:22.969
for white, and this queen is retreating to
only take up a better post. She eventually

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:28.909
wants to rake the 5th rank. All of the white
rooks are working for white now. And with

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:35.409
queen to d2 there is this common knight pivot
that is available, exploiting the queen's

00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:41.519
unprotected nature. Notice how if b5, there
could be knight d5. There's no time to take

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:46.769
the knight. The queens are in tension. Queen
on a5 is unprotected. If you take the queen,

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:51.680
you don't take back right away. You take the
piece with check. And only then take the

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.920
queen back. And then white is doing quite
well reinforcing c4. The one thing you really

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.569
don't want to happen structure-wise on the
queenside for white is this sort of thing

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:08.189
where they're split. But clearly in this variation
the rooks are well positioned to keep the

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:15.189
structure as is--an a-b structure. So we don't
have that. But that is the idea that is in

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:21.090
mind after queen d2. And the king gets out
of it. King to f8, so that knight d5 isn't

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:26.120
available. Queen takes queen. Well you can't
take a piece now with check. The king is on

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:33.120
f8. So king f8 it is. Queen to b2, and there
might be some issue on this g7 square. Well

00:04:33.229 --> 00:04:38.000
the king comes back. And during the live stream
I had thought that maybe we would have some

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.050
repetition. The queen going back and forth,
the king going back and forth. But certainly

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:47.300
Anand is looking for more. It may seem like
this is appealing to take that pawn and then

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.590
land a discovered check, but it's just not
working out. Black at the end of it is up

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:58.590
a piece. So what did we have? We had a4, clamping
down on this b5 advance. Queen to h5, knight

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:08.430
to e2. Two possibilities for the knight to
maybe harass the black queen. Bishop f6, and

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:13.060
now at this point now that the bishops are
in tension with one another. As soon as he's

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:18.669
no longer around to watch over d6. Well you're
going to have a lot of pressure being placed

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:23.889
on that point. He's a number one defender
of the d6 pawn. So he is challenged right

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:30.889
away. He's recaptured as otherwise you would
be dropping the rook. You need to take with

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:37.500
the rook, and after queen to e5 the idea will
be to eventually question the queen's position.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:43.720
First piling up on d6. Knight f6 is there.
There's pressure now on e4. That is defended,

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:49.710
and there is also no thought of d5. The queen
would be hit. And so what gets played after

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:56.710
rook e3, we have rook d7. And now a very clamping
down type of move, a5. You could forget about

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:02.610
playing b5. The queen is there to support
keep in mind. And now we have after queen

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:09.389
g5 an evaluation shift. What's maybe better,
and this is a computer recommendation is g5,

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:14.750
to secure this queen position. But instead
in the game, after a5, it was queen g5 which

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:19.419
now allows e5, breaking open the position.
Hitting at these two points. The knight has

00:06:19.419 --> 00:06:26.000
to run in reverse in order to defend d6. Now
in the game we had pawn takes pawn, but what

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.229
was available was also knight g3, or maybe
even knight c3 with ideas of hitting the queen

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:38.110
and then also converging on d6. However what
was chosen is the immediate capture. And this

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:43.860
pawn needs to be tracked down right away.
If something like b4-c5 is allowed and that

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:50.270
pawn is defended, with that sort of structure,
well it's going to be game over for black.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:56.150
So it's rook c6. f4 first, and we're going
down a line now where we're going to have

00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:02.389
all major exchanged, and we're going to enter
a knight, king and pawn endgame alone. So

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:09.389
f4 is first inserted. The queen needs to drop
back to corral the pawn on d6. And we have

00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:16.759
everything trained on this point, and sure
enough all major pieces coming off the board.

00:07:16.759 --> 00:07:20.990
And it was important by the way in all of
those cases that the knight would be the last

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:26.750
one to capture as otherwise there are moments
where c5 could be played, exploiting the pin

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:32.099
on the d-file. So that's why we have that
exact order coming about. And the knight being

00:07:32.099 --> 00:07:37.789
the last one to recapture. And now in this
endgame, the idea is to get the king more

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:44.050
active. And very active is this white king
with these next moves, making a b-line right

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:50.590
for the b-pawn. Black is there however, just
in the nick of time to stop white from putting

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:57.590
pressure on b7. So what follows is getting
the knight to a better post. An even post

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:04.939
on e4, looking at the d6 square, hitting at
f7. Also looking for some fork. Maybe picking

00:08:04.939 --> 00:08:11.939
up the g-pawn. Knight e3, g3. A question is
thrown to the knight. He does run into d6.

00:08:13.349 --> 00:08:18.930
And after g5, we give a check, and another
check. And now this where there was a big

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:25.340
think for Carlsen having about 61 minutes
remaining on this clock. He invested a good

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:30.750
chunk of that--nearly a half hour spent on
knight g8. And you may wonder why so much

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:37.750
time was invested on such a move. Well look
at the variation that comes about where after

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:43.919
takes, takes, king g7. The knight--he is trapped.
White is giving up the knight in exchange

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:50.919
for tracking down these pawns, and then having
a foot race. Literally a foot solider race

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:58.500
to see who can queen first. So he doesn't
just want to give himself up. He needs to

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.700
take somebody down with him. He grabs a pawn
at least on f5, and then the king hunts down

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.990
the b-pawn. Whereas the knight tracks down
the f-pawn. And before you know it we have

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:13.440
two passed pawns. King b6 is there clearing
the way for the pawn, and also shouldering

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:19.480
off the knight. It's a big race, and at the
end of it we have both sides queening. White

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:25.480
queen's first, and then takes up a nice residence
in the center. Even without these pawns on

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:31.010
the board, it's still a very difficult win
for black. Queen and knight versus queen should

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:38.010
be a theoretical draw but we have the game
actually ending much more abruptly. Keep in

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:43.800
mind that you can't, at this point, take the
pawn. She has to watch over the knight. So

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:50.800
what is tried is a knight check, king b5.
Knight takes on b3. The queen now is able

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.240
to give a check, and at this moment I was
able to notice that the game would be ending

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.740
very soon. Because by force it would either
be a perpetual check or the queens would be

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:09.740
exchanged. And that right there would secure
a draw. So what was played is king to h6.

00:10:11.649 --> 00:10:17.700
And what was the follow up? Queen b6, forcing
a queen exchange. What else would there have

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:23.550
been to do? Going to one of these squares...well
the queen basically keeps making use of these

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:30.550
three squares. Wherever the king goes, you
continually give a check until he has to go.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:36.600
Well it's either perpetual check or he ends
up somewhere on this 6th rank. So what was

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:43.600
played is queen c7, king h6, queen b6, and
the queens are exchanged. And with that, well

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:50.820
the game finishes up like this, with the pawns
being captured. And at this moment right here

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:56.139
we go all the way down to move 65, knight
takes pawn. And it is at this point it is

00:10:56.139 --> 00:11:02.680
of course a draw. No side can win, and with
that the World Chess Championship title goes

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:09.680
to Magnus Carlsen. So congratulations to him.
I've covered all games now. It feels good

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:16.490
to be done, and I hope you enjoyed tuning
for this. Maybe this is the first video you're

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:23.240
watching, but I covered all of these games.
All ten games of this match. And at the end

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:28.230
of this video, I'll include a playlist so
you can check them out if you missed any of

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:34.240
them. So thank you for tuning in. Thank you
for your support as well. And I hope you enjoyed

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:41.240
this World Chess Championship match, and again
congratulations to Magnus Carlsen. That's

00:11:41.279 --> 00:11:43.730
all for this video. Take care, bye.

